~ Assertions — the value under test first, a matcher second. Every assertion below holds,
~ so the program runs to completion and exits 0; a failing one reports at its own call site
~ and exits 101. `assert` needs no import: the compiler provides it, and the matchers with it.
~
~   assert(actual, equals(expected))  ~ fail unless `actual == expected`
~   assert(actual, contains(part))    ~ a part of a Text, or an element of an array
~   assert(actual, not(matcher))      ~ negation, around any matcher
~   assert(result, isOk())            ~ a Result is Ok / NotOk
~
~ `expect(actual, matcher)` is the same vocabulary for a test case: it records the failure
~ with the run instead of exiting, so the suite carries on. See examples/test_suite.qn.

Version = {
  major :: Num,
  minor :: Num,
  == = (other :: Version) -> Bool => < it.major == other.major && it.minor == other.minor >,
  ` = => < "v`it.major`.`it.minor`" >
}

^ = () -> $ => <
  ~ Equality over each built-in type, through the same `==` the operator uses.
  assert(6 * 7, equals(42))
  assert("qui" + "lon", equals("quilon"))
  assert(2 < 3, equals(true))

  ~ And over a user type, through the `==` member it declares. A failure renders both
  ~ values with the type's own `` ` ``.
  assert(Version { major = 0, minor = 9 }, equals(Version { major = 0, minor = 9 }))

  ~ Containment: a substring of a Text, an element of an array.
  assert("assertions and matchers", contains("matcher"))
  assert([2, 4, 6], contains(4))

  ~ Negation wraps any matcher.
  assert(1, not(equals(2)))
  assert("a", not(equals("b")))
  assert([2, 4, 6], not(contains(5)))

  ~ Results, via the absent-safe `at` (Ok in bounds, NotOk out of bounds).
  assert([10, 20, 30].at(0), isOk())
  assert([10, 20, 30].at(9), isNotOk())
>
